# The lambda for the pre-breeding model is the same as the post-breeding model
# Chunk 20
# eigen(X) # this function calculates the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix X
# eigen(X)$values # this command will just return the eigenvalues
# Re(eigen(X)$values) # this will return just the real parts of the eigenvalues (all of them but the first one are complex numbers)
# max(Re(eigen(X)$values)) # this will return just the first, dominant eigenvalue
# Change post-breeding transition matrix
ann_surv <- data.frame(new = 0.5,
one = 0.75,
two = 0.75,
three = 0.75,
adult = 0.8)
L[2, 1] <- ann_surv$new
L[1, 5] <- adult_fert
# Calculate eigenvalue
eigen(L)$values[1]
eigen(L_pre)$values[1]
## They are the same as the lambda
# Chunk 21
# To do this you will need to use eigen() to calculate the eigenvectors AND eigenvalues. Each eigenvector corresponds to an eigenvalue, listed in the same order. So find the eigenvalue in the list that is the largest one (usually R lists it first); the corresponding eigenvector is the dominant eigenvector. So usually it will be
# Eigenvec = eigen(X)$vectors[,1]. # but look at all of them first to be sure!
# To normalize the eigenvector, divide each value by the sum of the entire vector. If the original eigenvector had negative values, that's fine (sometimes this happens); this will turn them into positive proportions.
# Find pre-breeding eigenvector
pre_stable_age <- eigen(L_pre)$vectors[, 1]/
sum(eigen(L_pre)$vectors[, 1])
pre_stable_age #The pre-breeding model eigenvector
# Compare
pre_AgeStr <- proportions(N, 2)
pre_AgeStr[,31]
# Find post-breeding eigenvector
post_stable_age <- eigen(L)$vectors[, 1]/
sum(eigen(L)$vectors[, 1])
post_stable_age #The post-breeding model eigenvector
# Compare
stable_age_dist_orig
# The eigenvectors of the two models were nearly identical to the final proportions vector pulled from each respective proportions matrix. Any deviations were \<.0001.
